 # A budget a slow machine cannot fail
 
-"It got slower" is the review comment nobody can act on, and the obvious answer to it — time the
-thing — is a measurement of the machine that ran it. A wall-clock budget on a shared build machine is
-red on a busy afternoon and green on a quiet one, and the reflex it teaches is to re-run the job until
-it passes. That is a gate nobody reads.
+"It got slower" is the review comment nobody can act on, and the obvious answer to it — time the thing
+— is a measurement of the machine that ran it. A wall-clock budget on a shared build machine is red
+on a busy afternoon and green on a quiet one, and the reflex it teaches is to re-run the job until it
+passes. That is a gate nobody reads.
 
-<!-- TODO: say what this budget counts instead, and why those numbers are the same on every machine.
-Then say what the workload is: how many rows, in what order, and how many of them are not due yet —
-a queue that is entirely due is the easy half of the measurement. -->
+The budget on this page counts **work instead of time**: the rows a drain looked at, the rows it
+handed out, and how the first of those grows when the queue doubles. Every one of those numbers is
+the same on a loaded machine as on an idle one, in this process and in the next one, so the answer is
+about the change rather than about the load.
 
 ## What we measure
 
+The workload is one batch of **256 queue rows**, in the order a store ranked them
+([`MemoryStore::due`](../src/engine/store/memory.rs) is where that order is decided), with **one row
+in four not due yet** — a queue that is entirely due is the easy half: the drain has nothing to look
+at and skip.
+
 | metric | what it counts | baseline |
 | --- | --- | --- |
-| `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` |
-| `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` |
-| `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` |
+| `claim_statements` | rows handed out — the write one claim costs a store | 192 |
+| `scan_cells` | rows looked at, including the ones that turned out not to be due | 256 |
+| `scale_ratio_max` | the work at twice the size over the work at one size: an upper bound | 2.2 |
+
+The two counts are taken from the **256-row** run and the guard from the pair, which is why the first
+two are 192 and 256: three of every four rows are due, and a drain that remembers where it stopped
+looks at each row once.
 
-<!-- TODO: one row per metric the harness prints, with the baseline
-`course/tracks/jobq/budget/3.22-bench-budget.toml` records for it — the names are the ones
-`jobq::engine::METRICS` lists, and the spec compares this page with that list. Say why there are two
-counts and not one: which defect shows in one of them and not the other. -->
+Two counters rather than one, because they answer different questions and the defect usually shows in
+exactly one of them: a drain that examines every row twice has twice the cells and the *same*
+statements, and a drain that hands the same row out twice is the opposite. A single total would only
+report that something changed.
 
 ## The guard
 
-<!-- TODO: what the third metric is, why it is an upper bound rather than a target, and what the
-drain it exists to catch costs on a short queue versus a long one. Say what `Work::scale_to` answers
-when there is nothing to compare with, and why that is not zero. -->
+`scale_ratio_max` is the metric this page exists for. A drain that keeps its place costs about twice
+the work when the input doubles; a drain that starts the search over on every pull costs **the
+square**: on a sixteen-row batch those two are 16 cells and 112, and 3.7 times the work when the batch
+doubles — a difference nobody sees until the queue is long, which is exactly when it matters.
+
+It is an **upper bound, not a target**. A drain that got cheaper passes; a drain that costs 2.2 times
+as much per doubling is the point at which somebody has to explain why. The bound is a tenth over the
+two a linear drain costs, which is room for a per-batch constant rather than for a change of shape.
+
+`Work::scale_to` answers `None` when the smaller drain examined nothing, and the report leaves the
+guard's line out rather than printing a zero: a `0` would compare with the bound like any other ratio
+and report a *pass* for a measurement that never happened.
 
 ## How this is checked
 
-<!-- TODO: the command that prints the numbers, the gate that compares them with the baseline, and
-what the gate answers for a metric the harness did not print. Link the harness and the test that
-pins the counts: [the harness](TODO), [the step's test](TODO). -->
+The harness is [`tests/budget.rs`](../tests/budget.rs) — a file this project's course ships, because
+a learner changing the instrument they are measured with is a learner changing the measurement. It
+prints one `jobq-budget <name>=<value>` line per metric:
+
+```sh
+cargo test -p jobq --test budget -- --nocapture
+```
+
+`kiln check jobq/3.22-bench-budget --slow` runs exactly that command and compares each number with the
+baseline the course keeps in `course/tracks/jobq/budget/3.22-bench-budget.toml`: every metric within
+±10%, and a name ending in `_max` only *downwards*. A metric the harness does not print is `blocked`
+rather than a pass, and a run that prints nothing comparable is not judged at all — the harness is
+course content, so what a broken instrument means is that the gate cannot answer, not that this
+project failed.
+
+The workload's size is content rather than a knob: changing it changes what the baselines mean, so it
+is a decision somebody makes in the same commit as the numbers. The step's own test
+([`tests/spec_budget.rs`](../tests/spec_budget.rs)) pins both: the counts the baselines were measured
+from, the three lines the harness prints, and the two drains that show what the guard is for.
 
 ## Where this comes from
 
-<!-- TODO: what a wall-clock benchmark has to do to be trustworthy, and one harness built on counts
-for the reason this page gives. -->
+- [Criterion.rs: analysis](https://bheisler.github.io/criterion.rs/book/analysis.html) — what a
+  wall-clock benchmark has to do to be trustworthy (warm-up, sample size, outliers), and therefore
+  how much machinery a gate that depends on time needs;
+- [`iai`](https://github.com/bheisler/iai) — a benchmark harness built on instruction counts for
+  exactly the reason this page gives: it compares two versions of the code rather than two machines;
+- [Google SRE book: monitoring distributed systems](https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/)
+  — the same argument one level up, about why a metric a dashboard charts has to mean the same thing
+  on every machine that reports it.
